# PyStreamFlow reverse proxy - terminates HTTPS so the editor UI, the main
# HTTP API, and /mcp/* (LM Studio, Claude Desktop, or any other MCP client)
# are all reachable over a real TLS connection instead of plain HTTP.
#
# `mcp.lan` (replace with whatever hostname you actually use - see
# docker-compose.yml's own comment) isn't a publicly-registered domain, so
# Caddy can't get a Let's Encrypt certificate for it and automatically falls
# back to its own internal CA instead - this "just works" with zero config
# here, but every client that connects (a browser, LM Studio, curl) will see
# an untrusted-certificate warning until that CA is trusted. See
# docs/user_guide.md's "HTTPS via Caddy" section for how to extract and
# install it - a one-time step per client device, not something to redo on
# every restart (the `caddy_data` volume below is what makes the same CA/
# certs survive a restart instead of being regenerated).
#
# flush_interval -1 forces Caddy to flush every write to the client
# immediately instead of buffering - not optional here: /mcp/sse (the
# legacy SSE transport) and /mcp's own streamable-HTTP transport are both
# real long-lived streams, and buffering them would reintroduce the exact
# kind of "client connects but nothing ever arrives" symptom this project
# already spent a lot of effort chasing down at the application layer.
mcp.lan {
	reverse_proxy pystreamflow:8000 {
		flush_interval -1
	}
}
